In this lesson we'll look at equivalent fractions and how to place them on a number line. Two fractions are equivalent when they name the same amount, even if they look different — you can think of them as sitting at the exact same spot on a number line. To build a bigger version of a fraction, you multiply the top and the bottom by the same number, like turning one third into four twelfths by multiplying by four. To shrink a fraction back down, you do the reverse — divide the top and bottom by a common factor, so eight twelfths becomes two thirds when you divide by four. A fraction is in simplest form when the only number that divides evenly into both the top and the bottom is one. You can also mix whole numbers with fractions to get a mixed numeral, like two and three fifths, which is really just two plus three fifths. To switch between mixed numerals and improper fractions, remember the little tricks: multiply and add to go up, divide and find the remainder to come back down. Finally, the number line is just a straight line with equally spaced marks. Whatever sits further to the right is the bigger number, and you place a fraction by splitting the gap between two whole numbers into the right number of equal parts. Now let's see it in action.